WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:12.270
<v Ira Glass>There's this guy who did this thing that's extraordinary. He tried to live like a badger. And I say that in this wasn't a stunt, and he's not a crazy person. This was a sincere, thoughtful attempt to figure something out.
<v Ira Glass>有个人做了一件非同寻常的事。他尝试像獾一样生活。我这么说并非指这是一种噱头，他也不是个疯子。这是一个真诚且经过深思熟虑的尝试，为了弄明白一些事情。

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:35.160
<v Ira Glass>The guy's name is Charles Foster. And his interest in this kind of thing really all began when he was a kid. He says a blackbird used to come into his family's suburban backyard. And he would look at it through the window of his house and stare in its eye, and it would look back at him. And he would think to himself, I know this backyard so well, but the bird knows stuff about it I don't. The bird knows all kinds of things I don't.
<v Ira Glass>这个人名叫查尔斯·福斯特。他对这类事情的兴趣实际上始于他小时候。他说，过去常有一只黑鸫来到他家郊区的后院。他会透过家里的窗户看着它，凝视它的眼睛，它也会回望他。他会心想，我对这个后院了如指掌，但那只鸟却知道一些我不知道的事情。那只鸟知道各种我不知道的东西。

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.250
<v Charles Foster>And I wanted to know what that was.
<v Charles Foster>而我想知道那是什么。

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:48.860
<v Ira Glass>And you write that you went to the library, reading everything you could on it. You mapped all the nests in your area. And you would visit them every day. And you took notes. You had a notebook where you were basically gathering your field notes.
<v Ira Glass>你写道，你去了图书馆，阅读了所有能找到的相关资料。你绘制了你所在地区所有的巢穴地图。你每天都会去探访它们。而且你做了笔记。你有一个笔记本，基本上是在收集你的实地考察记录。

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:58.190
<v Charles Foster>Yeah. I was a fanatical note-taker, a fanatical mapper. I did things which, no doubt, would sound really strange or macabre now.
<v Charles Foster>是的。我是一个狂热的笔记记录者和测绘者。我所做的事情，现在听起来无疑是非常奇怪或阴森的。

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:02.350
<v Ira Glass>Like for instance, he taxidermied a dead blackbird he found. As a kid, he did this.
<v Ira Glass>例如，他把自己发现的一只死黑鸫制成了标本。小时候，他做过这种事。

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:10.580
<v Charles Foster>Mounted with its wings outspread, and it dangled over my bed. I used to watch it circling as I went to sleep at night.
<v Charles Foster>把它翅膀展开安装好，让它悬挂在我的床上方。晚上睡觉时，我常看着它盘旋。

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:11.280
<v Ira Glass>Hm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:20.790
<v Charles Foster>So yeah. And also I pickled in formalin a blackbird's brain and put it in a jar, and I would sometimes go to bed holding it in my hand.
<v Charles Foster>所以，是的。我还用福尔马林腌制了一只黑鸫的脑子，把它装进罐子里，有时我会握着它睡觉。

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:30.640
<v Ira Glass>He'd peer into it the way that you do when you're a kid, imagining, hoping something of the bird would seep into him. He'd fall asleep wishing he'd be a blackbird in his dreams.
<v Ira Glass>他会像孩子那样盯着它看，想象着，希望那只鸟的一部分能渗入到自己体内。他会带着希望在梦中成为黑鸫的愿望入睡。

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.840
<v Ira Glass>And as an adult, his fascination with animals continued. He became a veterinarian.
<v Ira Glass>成年后，他对动物的痴迷仍在继续。他成为了一名兽医。

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:41.720
<v Ira Glass>Can I ask, when you became a vet, was part of it because you wanted to get closer to animals? You wanted to understand them better?
<v Ira Glass>我可以问一下吗，当你成为兽医时，部分原因是因为你想更接近动物吗？你想更好地了解它们吗？

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:42.830
<v Charles Foster>It was exactly that.
<v Charles Foster>正是如此。

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:43.560
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:59.510
<v Charles Foster>I felt that, by putting my hands physically on animals, by knowing how they worked as machines, I would understand better those really mystical questions which I posed myself as a child.
<v Charles Foster>我觉得，通过亲手接触动物，通过了解它们作为机器是如何运作的，我能更好地理解那些我在孩童时期向自己提出的非常神秘的问题。

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:09.480
<v Ira Glass>Didn't work. If anything, he says, understanding animals as machines took him even further from the thing he was interested in. How do they perceive the world? What do they know?
<v Ira Glass>没用。他说，如果说有什么的话，那就是把动物理解为机器让他离他感兴趣的东西更远了。它们是如何感知世界的？它们知道些什么？

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:44.150
<v Ira Glass>And so, as an adult, he began to venture out into the woods to try to live as different animals. He documented this in a book called Being a Beast . The most interesting section in this book is his attempt to live like a badger. Usually he would do this alone, though for a few days he went with his eight-year-old son, Tom. On a friend's farm, they made a human-sized badger home. This was a tunnel, 15-feet long, that they would sleep in. Charles says he's probably spent six weeks living underground like this over the years, sleeping during the day, awake at night like real badgers.
<v Ira Glass>因此，成年后，他开始冒险进入森林，尝试像不同的动物一样生活。他在一本名为《像野兽一样》的书中记录了这一点。书中这部分最有趣的内容是他尝试像獾一样生活。通常他独自进行，不过有几天他和八岁的儿子汤姆一起。在朋友的农场里，他们造了一个人大小的獾穴。这是一个15英尺长的隧道，他们就在里面睡觉。查尔斯说，这些年来他大概花了六周时间像这样生活在地下，白天睡觉，晚上像真正的獾一样活动。

00:02:44.150 --> 00:03:03.940
<v Ira Glass>And the main part of living like a badger was being low to the ground, crawling around on their hands and knees, which he knows how weird that sounds. And they did something that's so simple but changes everything. Instead of using their eyes to get around-- badgers' eyesight is terrible-- they'd blindfold themselves and use their noses.
<v Ira Glass>而像獾一样生活的主要部分是身体贴近地面，手膝着地爬行，他知道这听起来有多奇怪。而且他们做了一件非常简单但改变了一切的事情。他们没有用眼睛来观察周围——獾的视力很差——而是蒙上眼睛，改用鼻子。

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:20.190
<v Charles Foster>When you move your nose over a small area of the forest floor, just move it an inch and you get a completely different set of smells. The ground is much, much more interesting when you smell it than when you look at it.
<v Charles Foster>当你把鼻子移过森林地面的一小块区域时，只需移动一英寸，你就会闻到完全不同的一系列气味。当你用嗅觉感知地面时，它比你用眼睛去看要有趣得多、多得多。

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:28.000
<v Ira Glass>And then everything changes when rain comes. Just describe how different the entire place is when rain finally hits.
<v Ira Glass>而且下雨时一切都会改变。描述一下当雨水真正落下时，整个地方有多么不同。

00:03:28.000 --> 00:04:01.930
<v Charles Foster>Rain unlocks scent. So there's a massive explosion for olfactory animals of the intensity of the place. It was like the volume suddenly being turned on. And not just volume. It was a better sort of music. It was like switching to Mozart after you'd been having the piped music in the mall. It was incredibly exciting.
<v Charles Foster>雨水释放了气味。所以对于依靠嗅觉的动物来说，这地方的气味强度会产生巨大的爆发。就像音量突然被调大了一样。而且不仅仅是音量。那是更好的一种音乐。就像你一直在商场里听背景音乐，突然切换到了莫扎特。这令人难以置信地兴奋。

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:17.080
<v Ira Glass>He says it only took a few days for him and his son to be able to navigate the landscape in the woods blindfolded, using only their noses. Oak trees were good landmarks, he says, because surprisingly, they all smell different from each other. I asked him to read this passage from the book describing what the oak smelled like on those outings.
<v Ira Glass>他说，他和儿子只用了几天时间，就能蒙着眼睛，仅靠鼻子在森林里导航。他说，橡树是很好的地标，因为令人惊讶的是，它们每棵闻起来都不一样。我让他读了书中描述那些短途旅行中橡树气味的一段。

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:29.930
<v Charles Foster>"Out of the tunnel, turn right. 15 yards; raw tobacco, mostly Turkish; straight on. After half a minute, wall of limes and sick in front."
<v Charles Foster>“出洞口，右转。15码；生烟草味，主要是土耳其烟草；直走。半分钟后，面前是一堵酸橙和呕吐物的墙。”

00:04:29.930 --> 00:05:01.870
<v Charles Foster>"If I had to pick one word for the badger's experience, it would be 'intimate.' Grass and bracken stems brush your face. When you're forcing a new path, the stems graze. Water shudders off grass into your eyes. Things slide away. We bustled and grunted and elbowed and pushed and pressed our noses into the ground. And even we smelled something-- the citrusy piss of the voles; the distantly maritime tang of a slug trail, like a winter rock pool; the sharp musk of a weasel; the blunter musk of an otter."
<v Charles Foster>“如果非要用一个词来形容獾的体验，那应该是‘亲密’。草和蕨类植物的茎刷过你的脸。当你强行开辟一条新路时，茎部会擦伤你。水从草叶上颤抖着落入你的眼睛。东西滑走。我们忙碌着、咕哝着、用肘部推挤，并将鼻子压入地面。即使是我们也能闻到一些东西——田鼠那柑橘味的小便；蛞蝓痕迹那遥远的海洋气息，就像冬天的岩石水池；鼬鼠那尖锐的麝香味；水獭那更沉闷的麝香味。”

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:13.380
<v Ira Glass>It got to the point where his eight-year-old son one day declared, I can smell mice, and then he headed off into the distance and found-- OK, not mice, but another kind of rodent-- bank voles.
<v Ira Glass>以至于有一天他八岁的儿子宣布，我能闻到老鼠的味道，然后他就向远处跑去，找到了——好吧，不是老鼠，而是另一种啮齿动物——红背䶄。

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:34.390
<v Ira Glass>I can't talk about this book without having Charles Foster read one more excerpt. Badgers eat earthworms, so he felt compelled to at least try them. And he tried a bunch, from all over. He would put them into his mouth alive and feel them try to escape by wriggling through the gaps in his teeth, and then he'd bite.
<v Ira Glass>如果不让查尔斯·福斯特再读一段摘录，我就无法谈论这本书。獾吃蚯蚓，所以他觉得必须至少尝尝它们。他试过很多种，来自各地。他会把活蚯蚓放进嘴里，感受它们试图通过牙缝扭动逃跑，然后他就咬下去。

00:05:34.390 --> 00:06:00.060
<v Charles Foster>"Earthworms taste of slime and the land. Worms from Chablis have a long, mineral finish. Worms from Picardy are musty; they taste of decay and splintered wood. Worms from the high Kent Weald are fresh and uncomplicated; they'd appear in the list recommended with a grilled sole. About 85% of an average badger's diet is earthworms. This fact both drains badgers of some of their charisma and makes them excitingly inaccessible."
<v Charles Foster>“蚯蚓尝起来有黏液和泥土的味道。来自夏布利的蚯蚓余味悠长，有矿物质感。来自皮卡第的蚯蚓带有霉味；它们尝起来有腐烂和碎木头的味道。来自肯特高地威尔德的蚯蚓新鲜而纯粹；它们会出现在推荐搭配烤鳎鱼的菜单上。獾平均饮食中约85%是蚯蚓。这一事实既让獾失去了一些魅力，又让它们变得令人兴奋地难以捉摸。”

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:05.500
<v Ira Glass>Are those real flavors that you're describing, or are you just riffing on different potential tastes of the worms of Chablis?
<v Ira Glass>你描述的那些是真实的味道，还是你只是在随意联想夏布利蚯蚓可能具有的味道？

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:07.240
<v Charles Foster>No, no. Those are real flavors.
<v Charles Foster>不，不。那些是真实的味道。

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:10.300
<v Ira Glass>And then sometimes you would eat them raw and sometimes cooked?
<v Ira Glass>而且有时你会生吃它们，有时会煮熟吃？

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:19.210
<v Charles Foster>Yeah. And I recommend, if anyone wants to do this, that they cook them and they have them with garlic.
<v Charles Foster>是的。如果有人想尝试的话，我建议把它们煮熟，然后配上大蒜一起吃。

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:41.690
<v Ira Glass>Charles Foster isn't just a veterinarian. He's also a practicing lawyer, and he teaches at Oxford University in England. And when he writes about the question that is at the heart of his interest with animals-- the question, what can we really understand about an animal's experience-- he parses what we can and cannot know with a lawyer's precision. He is meticulous. And he has thought about this a lot.
<v Ira Glass>查尔斯·福斯特不仅是一名兽医。他还是一位执业律师，并在英国牛津大学任教。当他写到他关注动物的核心问题——即我们到底能理解多少动物的体验——时，他以律师的严谨剖析了我们能知和不能知的事物。他一丝不苟。而且他对此思考了很多。

00:06:41.690 --> 00:07:00.360
<v Ira Glass>He says, of course we can't know what animals think, how they conceive of themselves, how they conceive of the world. We can't really know what they feel. But with many animals, he says, we can know their physical experience because we share so much physiology with so many animals-- our limbs and eyes and noses and mouths and the way our nerves work.
<v Ira Glass>他说，我们当然无法知道动物在想什么，它们如何看待自己，如何看待世界。我们无法真正知道它们的感觉。但他说，对于许多动物，我们可以知道它们的身体体验，因为我们与许多动物共享太多的生理构造——我们的四肢、眼睛、鼻子、嘴巴以及我们神经运作的方式。

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:11.720
<v Ira Glass>And that's why he tried to live like animals live. Putting his own body, his own physiology, into a badger's landscape seemed like his best chance of getting a tiny glimpse of what it might feel like to be a badger.
<v Ira Glass>这就是为什么他尝试像动物一样生活。将他自己的身体、他自己的生理机能置于獾的景观中，似乎是他获得对獾的感觉有一丁点了解的最好机会。

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:19.040
<v Ira Glass>What are the moments that you feel like you got closest to the thing you were seeking?
<v Ira Glass>哪些时刻让你觉得最接近你所追求的东西？

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:38.600
<v Charles Foster>There was the moment of-- there was the moment when I said to myself, there's a tree there because there's the smell of a tree, rather than there's a tree there because I looked at it last Thursday and I know that it should be.
<v Charles Foster>有这样一个时刻——有这样一个时刻，我对自己说，那里有一棵树，是因为有树的味道，而不是因为上周四我看到了它，所以我知道它应该在那里。

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:53.240
<v Ira Glass>Mostly this did not work. He's the first to point that out. As soon as he would stand up and see the world again with his head 6 feet in the air, it all went away. The visual world reasserted itself. His head filled with thoughts that a badger would never think.
<v Ira Glass>大多数时候这并没有奏效。他是第一个指出这一点的人。一旦他站起来，头部距离地面6英尺，再次用眼睛看世界，一切就都消失了。视觉世界重新确立了地位。他的脑海里充满了獾永远不会想的思想。

00:07:53.240 --> 00:08:05.010
<v Ira Glass>But I could understand why he gave so many years to this project, even though he knew all along it was such a long shot. It's too exciting to leave yourself and try to be something so different.
<v Ira Glass>但我能理解他为什么在这个项目上投入了这么多年，尽管他从一开始就知道这希望渺茫。离开自己，试图成为某种完全不同的东西，这太让人兴奋了。

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:40.750
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, Becoming a Badger. We have two stories for you about trying to become something you are not. One of these stories is about a guy from France. One is about a dog. In each of the stories, they set out to become different from what they are, and they do it by just jumping into the new identity, into the new thing they want to be, pretending to be that thing, and hoping it sticks. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>好的，来自芝加哥WBEZ，这是《This American Life》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天我们的节目主题是“成为一只獾”。我们有两个关于尝试成为你本身以外的东西的故事。其中一个故事是关于一个来自法国的家伙。另一个是关于一条狗。在每个故事中，他们都着手去成为与自己不同的人，他们通过直接跳入新的身份，跳入他们想要成为的新事物，假装自己就是那个东西，并希望这能奏效。请留在我们身边。

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:45.370
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Je Suis Ici Toute La Semaine.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，Je Suis Ici Toute La Semaine。

00:08:45.370 --> 00:09:05.170
<v Ira Glass>So I heard about this guy. His name is Gad Elmaleh, and he's basically the most famous stand-up comedian in France. He plays arenas in France. He gets mobbed everywhere by fans and paparazzi, yadda, yadda. Yadda, yadda's actually appropriate. Gad is known as the Jerry Seinfeld of France. Just to give you a sense, this is the beginning of one of his most popular concert videos. It's from 2010.
<v Ira Glass>所以我听说过这个家伙。他的名字叫加德·艾尔马莱，他基本上是法国最著名的单口喜剧演员。他在法国的体育场演出。他到处被粉丝和狗仔队围堵，等等等等。其实用“等等等等”很合适。加德被称为法国的杰瑞·宋飞。为了让你有个概念，这是他最受欢迎的演唱会视频之一的开头。那是2010年的。

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:07.670
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "DON'T STOP 'TIL YOU GET ENOUGH" BY MICHAEL JACKSON]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 迈克尔·杰克逊的《DON'T STOP 'TIL YOU GET ENOUGH》]

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:09.660
<v Ira Glass>[CROWD SCREAMING]
<v Ira Glass>[人群尖叫]

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:23.640
<v Ira Glass>Yes, that's a Michael Jackson song they got the rights to. Gad does this rock star thing where his silhouette hits the stage before he does, and then his silhouette dances around to the music, tantalizing the crowd until the spotlight hits him.
<v Ira Glass>是的，那是他们获得了版权的迈克尔·杰克逊的歌。加德在做这种摇滚明星范儿的事情，他的剪影先于他本人出现在舞台上，然后他的剪影随着音乐跳舞，撩拨着人群，直到聚光灯打在他身上。

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:27.140
<v Gad Elmaleh>Bonsoir!
<v Gad Elmaleh>晚上好！

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:45.040
<v Ira Glass>Here's how famous he is. His initial jokes are about being famous-- how freaked out his fans get, meeting somebody as famous as he is. But about a year ago, Gad embarked on this quest. He decided he was going to try to make it as a successful comic here in America in English.
<v Ira Glass>他有多出名呢。他最初的笑话是关于自己出名的——他的粉丝在见到像他这么出名的人时有多惊慌。但大约一年前，加德踏上了这次探索。他决定他要尝试在美国用英语成为一名成功的喜剧演员。

00:09:45.040 --> 00:10:06.950
<v Ira Glass>This is an incredibly difficult and totally unnecessary thing for anybody to try to do. In France, everybody knows Gad Elmaleh. It's going great for him. And he's giving all that up to start again at the bottom, doing small clubs and venues. He has to reinvent how he does his whole job. And he's struggling. It's simultaneously audacious and very humble.
<v Ira Glass>对任何人来说，这都是一件极其困难且完全没必要的事情。在法国，每个人都认识加德·艾尔马莱。他在那里发展得很好。他却放弃了这一切，从底层重新开始，在小型俱乐部和场馆演出。他必须彻底重新发明他做这份工作的方式。他正在挣扎。这既大胆又非常谦逊。

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:37.470
<v Ira Glass>I have to say, I'm kind of a sucker for somebody who decides to do something really hard that nobody wants but them just because they can't help themselves. And that is what this is. And what is funny in France to French people, you will not be surprised to hear, is not the same as what is funny in English to American people. So to succeed at this, Gad has to understand the exact contours of what is different between the sense of humor of these two entirely different countries while he's on stage. He has to do this live while trying to entertain paying customers.
<v Ira Glass>我不得不说，我有点偏爱那种因为无法控制自己，而决定去做一些没人想要但只有他们自己想做的非常困难的事情的人。而这正是现在的状况。而在法国让法国人觉得好笑的东西，不用说，你也会猜到，和在美国让美国人觉得好笑的东西是不一样的。所以为了成功，加德必须在台上的时候，准确地理解这两个完全不同的国家的幽默感之间的细微差别。他必须在现场尝试娱乐付费观众的同时做到这一点。

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:45.550
<v Ira Glass>Reporter Zack McDermott is the one who first told us about Gad. Zack is a comedy nerd and a former stand-up himself. He takes the story from here.
<v Ira Glass>记者扎克·麦克德莫特是第一个告诉我们加德情况的人。扎克是个喜剧书呆子，以前自己也是做单口喜剧的。他从这里开始讲述这个故事。

00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:56.050
<v Zack McDermott>The only reason I know who Gad Elmaleh is is because of my wife. She's Belgian. I'll call her Aurelie because, although we've been married for three years, I still can't pronounce her name right.
<v Zack McDermott>我之所以知道加德·艾尔马莱是谁，是因为我妻子。她是比利时人。我会称呼她为奥蕾莉，因为尽管我们已经结婚三年了，但我仍然无法准确地读出她的名字。

00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:56.950
<v Aurelie>Aurelie.
<v Aurelie>奥蕾莉。

00:10:56.950 --> 00:10:57.960
<v Zack McDermott>Once again.
<v Zack McDermott>再说一次。

00:10:57.960 --> 00:10:58.880
<v Aurelie>Aurelie.
<v Aurelie>奥蕾莉。

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:02.940
<v Zack McDermott>Anyway. For her, Gad Elmaleh was a household name growing up.
<v Zack McDermott>总之。对她来说，加德·艾尔马莱是她成长过程中的家喻户晓的名字。

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:17.820
<v Aurelie>And you know, it's funny because there are a few of his jokes that are so famous that if you say one sentence, everybody knows that you're referring to him. I thought about that. There is something about learning English, and the sentence Brian is in the kitchen--
<v Aurelie>而且你知道，这很有趣，因为他有几个笑话太出名了，如果你说出一句话，每个人都知道你指的是他。我思考过这个问题。学习英语这件事有点东西，还有“布莱恩在厨房里”那句话——

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:25.160
<v Zack McDermott>It's a routine he does about learning English as a kid and the random phrases he had to repeat over and over again, like, where is Brian?
<v Zack McDermott>这是他关于小时候学英语的一个段子，以及他不得不一遍又一遍重复的随机短语，比如，“布莱恩在哪里？”

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:29.940
<v Gad Elmaleh>Where is Brian? [FRENCH], Brian is in the kitchen.
<v Gad Elmaleh>“布莱恩在哪里？”[法语]，布莱恩在厨房里。

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:32.730
<v Gad Elmaleh>[LAUGHTER]
<v Gad Elmaleh>[笑声]

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:44.130
<v Aurelie>I mean, I remember my friends, when I was maybe like 15, 16, or maybe a little older-- everybody was saying that. It's like, what is it in English? Like, that's what she said. It's like that. Everybody says that.
<v Aurelie>我的意思是，我记得我的朋友们，在我大概15、16岁的时候，或者可能大一点的时候——每个人都在说那个。这就像，英语里是什么？就像“那是她说的”。就像那样。每个人都这么说。

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:44.980
<v Sean Cole>That's what she said.
<v Sean Cole>“那是她说的”。

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:45.820
<v Aurelie>Yeah.
<v Aurelie>是的。

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:50.290
<v Zack McDermott>That's my producer, Sean Cole, by the way. You'll be hearing his voice a bit, too, as we go.
<v Zack McDermott>顺便说一下，那是我的制作人肖恩·科尔。随着节目的进行，你也会听到他的声音。

00:11:50.290 --> 00:12:07.400
<v Zack McDermott>So Aurelie was really excited when Gad moved to New York and started doing shows here. She drug me to a couple of them, including a very early gig last September at a performance space called Joe's Pub. It's not a bar. It's kind of a highbrow New York Cabaret space, and it's cozy. Seats less than 200.
<v Zack McDermott>所以当加德搬到纽约并开始在这里演出时，奥蕾莉真的很兴奋。她硬拉着我去看他的几场演出，包括去年九月在一家名为乔酒吧的表演空间里进行的一场非常早期的演出。那不是酒吧。那更像是一个高雅的纽约歌舞空间，而且很舒适。座位不到200个。

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:15.660
<v Aurelie>Even just seeing him in such a small venue, it's such a treat. And it's like, wow, how lucky French people are. You're like, that's great.
<v Aurelie>即使只是在这么小的场馆见到他，也是一种享受。而且你会觉得，哇，法国人真幸运。你会觉得，那太棒了。

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:16.700
<v Sean Cole>I know. It's like seeing like--
<v Sean Cole>我知道。就像看到……

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:18.070
<v Aurelie>Sort of like VIP when you do that.
<v Aurelie>你做那种事的时候就像是贵宾。

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:19.010
<v Sean Cole>You feel like VIP.
<v Sean Cole>你感觉自己像贵宾。

00:12:19.010 --> 00:12:20.530
<v Aurelie>You really do, yeah.
<v Aurelie>真的，是的。

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:22.670
<v Sean Cole>It's like seeing Neil Young play at a bar or something.
<v Sean Cole>就像看到尼尔·杨在酒吧演出之类的。

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:24.190
<v Aurelie>I don't know who that is, but sure.
<v Aurelie>我不知道那是谁，但好吧。

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:26.740
<v Aurelie>[LAUGHTER]
<v Aurelie>[笑声]

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:31.380
<v Announcer>Joe's Pub is now proud to present Gad Elmaleh.
<v Announcer>乔酒吧现在很荣幸地呈现加德·艾尔马莱。

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:33.480
<v Announcer>[APPLAUSE]
<v Announcer>[掌声]

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:52.140
<v Zack McDermott>This was, hands down, the best-looking and best-dressed crowd for a comedy show I've ever seen. So many scarves. Such tight jeans. The women looked great, too. The room was mostly French, with some Moroccan fans spackled in, as well. Gad's ethnically Moroccan, and he grew up there, so most of the room knew his French act.
<v Zack McDermott>这绝对是我见过的喜剧表演中最好看、穿着最讲究的观众。围巾真多。牛仔裤真紧。女士们看起来也很棒。房间里大部分是法国人，零星夹杂着一些摩洛哥粉丝。加德是摩洛哥裔，他在那里长大，所以房间里大多数人都知道他的法语表演。

00:12:52.140 --> 00:13:02.950
<v Gad Elmaleh>Is he really gonna do this in English? Yeah! Oh, wow. I don't know why I'm doing this. I don't know. I don't know. I don't know.
<v Gad Elmaleh>他真的要用英语做这个吗？是的！哦，哇。我不知道我为什么要这样做。我不知道。我不知道。我不知道。

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:26.990
<v Zack McDermott>The first thing you notice about Gad is that he's got incredible stage presence. And he's a very physical comedian. One minute he's all elegance and grace, the next he chimps up his face and flops around. His English act is basically observational comedy, including a lot of outsiders' impressions of America and, specifically, New York. For instance, he says he likes to ask directions of Americans because they always include Starbucks.
<v Zack McDermott>关于加德，你注意到的第一件事是他有令人难以置信的舞台表现力。他是一位非常有肢体动作的喜剧演员。前一分钟他还是优雅和风度翩翩，下一分钟他就做出猴子脸，四处乱倒。他的英语表演基本上是观察式喜剧，包括很多局外人对美国，特别是纽约的印象。例如，他说他喜欢向美国人问路，因为他们总是会提到星巴克。

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:42.710
<v Gad Elmaleh>You want to go straight, then there's a Starbucks on your left. You make a right. The next block there's two Starbucks. You make another right. There's another block with another one Starbucks. Make a left at the other block. There's no Starbucks, so don't panic.
<v Gad Elmaleh>你想要直走，然后在你左边有个星巴克。你向右转。下一个街区有两个星巴克。你再向右转。下个街区还有一个星巴克。在另一个街区左转。没有星巴克，所以别慌。

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:46.280
<v Gad Elmaleh>[LAUGHTER]
<v Gad Elmaleh>[笑声]

00:13:46.280 --> 00:13:49.010
<v Gad Elmaleh>Asking directions in France is different.
<v Gad Elmaleh>在法国问路是不同的。

00:13:49.010 --> 00:13:59.950
<v Zack McDermott>And this bleeds into another staple of his show, which I'll call, Americans be like this, French people be like that. Here's a joke about shopping in New York and this one, over-the-top, earnest sales guy.
<v Zack McDermott>这又引出了他演出的另一个主打内容，我可以称之为“美国人是这样，法国人是那样”。这是一个关于在纽约购物以及这个超级夸张、一本正经的销售员的笑话。

00:13:59.950 --> 00:14:05.400
<v Gad Elmaleh>And he told me something you'll never hear in France. Let me double check.
<v Gad Elmaleh>他告诉了我一些你在法国永远听不到的话。让我仔细核对一下。

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:10.220
<v Gad Elmaleh>[LAUGHTER]
<v Gad Elmaleh>[笑声]

00:14:10.220 --> 00:14:14.090
<v Gad Elmaleh>In France, we don't even have single-check!
<v Gad Elmaleh>在法国，我们甚至连单人结账都没有！

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:29.950
<v Zack McDermott>You can tell from response in the room that this isn't exactly an away game for Gad. The French and Moroccan folks in the audience love him unconditionally, but even here, in front of the friendliest possible crowd, it's a rough show. There are jokes that just don't go anywhere.
<v Zack McDermott>你可以从房间里的反应看出，这对加德来说并不完全是一场客场比赛。观众中的法国和摩洛哥人无条件地爱他，但即使在这里，在面对最友好的观众时，这也是一场艰难的演出。有些笑话根本没人笑。

00:14:29.950 --> 00:14:33.730
<v Gad Elmaleh>A friend of mine, he said, I am a fundamentalist atheist--
<v Gad Elmaleh>我有个朋友，他说，我是个原教旨主义的无神论者——

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:35.450
<v Zack McDermott>A fundamentalist atheist.
<v Zack McDermott>一个原教旨主义的无神论者。

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:52.030
<v Gad Elmaleh>I said, what the [BLEEP] is that? It's like if you're ordering a triple-shot espresso, but decaf. I like this one, but I will have to work on it.
<v Gad Elmaleh>我说，那他妈的是什么？就像你点了一杯三份浓缩咖啡，但要无咖啡因的。我喜欢这个，但我得改进一下。

00:14:52.030 --> 00:15:01.570
<v Zack McDermott>He said this a few times. OK. I have to work on that joke. He had a notepad on his stool, and he'd occasionally jot down which jokes worked and which didn't.
<v Zack McDermott>他说了好几次。好的。我得改进那个笑话。他在凳子上放了一个笔记本，他偶尔会记下哪些笑话有效，哪些无效。

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:03.750
<v Gad Elmaleh>OK. Good joke, bad delivery.
<v Gad Elmaleh>好的。笑话很好，表达很烂。

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:05.850
<v Gad Elmaleh>[LAUGHTER]
<v Gad Elmaleh>[笑声]

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:13.660
<v Gad Elmaleh>It's OK. Let's be humble tonight. It's a work in progress. More work than progress, but it's OK.
<v Gad Elmaleh>没关系。今晚要谦虚一点。这是一个进行中的工作。进展少，工作多，但没关系。

00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:28.210
<v Zack McDermott>He's done other gigs in front of audiences that don't know him where he bombed. It's something that just doesn't happen to him in France these days. Imagine doing something 5,000 times, and on the 5,001st time, you suck. It's not a good feeling.
<v Zack McDermott>他在其他观众不认识他的地方演出过，那里他搞砸了。这在他现在法国简直不可能发生。想象一下你做过5000次某件事，在第5001次时，你却搞砸了。这感觉可不好。

00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:46.530
<v Gad Elmaleh>That feeling like if you wear a sweater and you're all wet on your body right after the shower. And you put the-- agh, eugh, algh. You just want to hide. Yeah, I bombed some nights.
<v Gad Elmaleh>那种感觉就像你穿着毛衣，刚洗完澡身体还是湿的。然后你穿上……啊，呃，哎。你只想躲起来。是的，我有些晚上搞砸了。

00:15:46.530 --> 00:16:05.220
<v Gad Elmaleh>To be honest, I had so many moments where I was, oh my god, how am I gonna do this? I had so many moments writing, crafting last-minute jokes in front of a club, alone, and like, what am I doing?
<v Gad Elmaleh>说实话，我有很多时刻觉得，天哪，我该怎么做？我有很多时刻在写剧本，在俱乐部前临时创作笑话，独自一人，心想，我在做什么？

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:20.930
<v Zack McDermott>Good question. Why is he doing this? Onstage, he jokes that he's doing it because he needed to challenge himself, which is only partly a joke. In France, he's reached this weird level of fame where his fans laugh at everything. He told me it doesn't matter what he says anymore.
<v Zack McDermott>好问题。他为什么要这么做？在台上，他开玩笑说他这么做是因为他需要挑战自己，这只是半开玩笑。在法国，他已经达到了那种奇怪的知名度，粉丝们对他说的任何话都笑。他告诉我，他说什么已经不重要了。

00:16:20.930 --> 00:16:30.100
<v Gad Elmaleh>With the people who know me, it's like, oh, heh. They always some, heh. And I don't like that.
<v Gad Elmaleh>对于认识我的人来说，就像，哦，呵。他们总是会，呵。我不喜欢那样。

00:16:30.100 --> 00:16:31.910
<v Sean Cole>They kind of give you a pass.
<v Sean Cole>他们算是给你面子。

00:16:31.910 --> 00:16:33.020
<v Gad Elmaleh>Yeah.
<v Gad Elmaleh>是的。

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:40.670
<v Zack McDermott>It's not satisfying, he says. It's too easy. But working here-- it's hard again, and that's what he likes about it.
<v Zack McDermott>他说，这不满足。太容易了。但在美国工作——又变得困难了，而这就是他喜欢的地方。

00:16:40.670 --> 00:17:04.830
<v Gad Elmaleh>I'm excited. I wake up in the morning. I can't wait for the night to perform exactly like it was 20 years ago when I began doing stand-up in France because it was new. Like everything, you know? Like relationship. Like a gum, you know? Gum-- you just put it in your mouth. It's full of sugar. This is now.
<v Gad Elmaleh>我很兴奋。我早上醒来。我迫不及待地想等到晚上去表演，就像我20年前在法国刚开始做单口喜剧时那样，因为它是新的。就像所有事情，你知道吗？就像一段关系。就像口香糖，你知道吗？口香糖——你刚放进嘴里。它充满了糖。这是现在。

00:17:04.830 --> 00:17:07.660
<v Sean Cole>Is that what happened in France? There was no more sugar in your gum?
<v Sean Cole>法国发生过那种事吗？你的口香糖里没糖了？

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:18.360
<v Gad Elmaleh>No more sugar in my gum. So we need to bring back the sugar in our gum.
<v Gad Elmaleh>我的口香糖里没糖了。所以我们需要把糖带回我们的口香糖里。

00:17:18.360 --> 00:17:29.840
<v Zack McDermott>But Gad's friend Dan Naturman believes there's another reason Gad wants to make it in English. Dan's one of the few comics who's gone the other direction. He's an American who sometimes performs in French-- in Paris and Montreal.
<v Zack McDermott>但加德的朋友丹·纳特曼认为，加德想用英语取得成功还有另一个原因。丹是少数反方向发展的喜剧演员之一。他是个美国人，有时用法语表演——在巴黎和蒙特利尔。

00:17:29.840 --> 00:18:06.110
<v Dan Naturman>For any French comic-- and I've spent enough time talking to them-- they don't feel like they've conquered comedy until they've conquered America, because this is perceived as the home of stand-up comedy. You ask a French comic, who are your idols, you're more likely to hear Louis CK, Chris Rock. So in that sense, he feels like, OK. I did well in France, but am I really a great comedian if I haven't conquered America? I think he's somewhat insecure in that regard. I think, deep in his heart, he's saying, but I'm not Louis CK. I'm not Seinfeld. I want to be those guys, or as close as I can get. And the only way to do that is to come here.
<v Dan Naturman>对于任何法国喜剧演员——我花了很多时间跟他们聊天——他们觉得除非征服了美国，否则就不算征服了喜剧，因为这里被视为单口喜剧的大本营。你问一个法国喜剧演员，谁是你的偶像，你更有可能听到路易·CK，克里斯·洛克。所以从这个意义上说，他觉得，好的。我在法国做得很好，但如果我没有征服美国，我是伟大的喜剧演员吗？我觉得他在那方面有点不安全感。我觉得，在他内心深处，他在说，但我不是路易·CK。我不是宋飞。我想成为那些人，或者尽可能接近他们。而做到这一点的唯一方法就是来这里。

00:18:06.110 --> 00:18:07.630
<v Zack McDermott>Can he get there?
<v Zack McDermott>他能做到吗？

00:18:07.630 --> 00:18:09.730
<v Dan Naturman>It's gonna be a rough one, isn't it?
<v Dan Naturman>这会很难，不是吗？

00:18:09.730 --> 00:18:12.610
<v Zack McDermott>We played a recording of that quote for Gad.
<v Zack McDermott>我们给加德播放了那段引语的录音。

00:18:12.610 --> 00:18:15.980
<v Gad Elmaleh>It's not easy to hear, but it doesn't hurt.
<v Gad Elmaleh>听起来不容易，但不伤人。

00:18:15.980 --> 00:18:16.950
<v Sean Cole>Is it true?
<v Sean Cole>是真的吗？

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:35.000
<v Gad Elmaleh>Yeah, it's true. Because if you're a great soccer player in America, you want to be with the Real de Madrid. You want to be with Barcelona. You want to be with Bayern de Munich. You want to be with Arsenal.
<v Gad Elmaleh>是的，是真的。因为如果你是美国的顶级足球运动员，你想去皇家马德里。你想去巴塞罗那。你想去拜仁慕尼黑。你想去阿森纳。

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:56.760
<v Zack McDermott>Yet someone who wants to connect with an American audience might choose a different analogy, but you get the point. Early on in this whole adventure, Gad made a documentary for French TV about moving to the States. In one scene, the French Jerry Seinfeld is sitting at a restaurant with one of the few people who understands exactly how difficult it will be to make it in America-- the American Jerry Seinfeld. They're friends.
<v Zack McDermott>然而，一个想与美国观众建立联系的人可能会选择不同的类比，但你明白了。在整个冒险的早期，加德为法国电视台拍摄了一部关于搬到美国的纪录片。在其中一个场景中，法国的杰瑞·宋飞正坐在餐厅里，和少数几个确切知道在美国取得成功有多困难的人之一——美国杰瑞·宋飞在一起。他们是朋友。

00:18:56.760 --> 00:19:03.090
<v Jerry Seinfeld>Let me tell you. Let me explain this to you. The comedian-- the stand-up comedian-- was invented in America.
<v Jerry Seinfeld>让我告诉你。让我跟你解释一下。喜剧演员——单口喜剧演员——是在美国发明的。

00:19:03.090 --> 00:19:03.470
<v Gad Elmaleh>Yeah.
<v Gad Elmaleh>是的。

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:04.380
<v Jerry Seinfeld>It was invented in America.
<v Jerry Seinfeld>是在美国发明的。

00:19:04.380 --> 00:19:07.920
<v Gad Elmaleh>So you're surprised that I came to America to do this?
<v Gad Elmaleh>所以你对我也来美国做这件事感到惊讶？

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:14.680
<v Jerry Seinfeld>I get what you're doing. It's like, I want to go build a car, but I want to build it in Germany.
<v Jerry Seinfeld>我明白你在做什么。这就像，我想去造一辆车，但我想要在德国造。

00:19:14.680 --> 00:19:15.230
<v Gad Elmaleh>OK.
<v Gad Elmaleh>好的。

00:19:15.230 --> 00:19:24.180
<v Jerry Seinfeld>And people say, really? In Germany? Then I'm going to go to Italy, and I'm gonna open up a pasta factory. And then I'm going to go to France. I want to make wine.
<v Jerry Seinfeld>然后人们会说，真的吗？在德国？然后我要去意大利，我要开一家意面工厂。然后我要去法国。我想酿酒。

00:19:24.180 --> 00:19:24.690
<v Gad Elmaleh>OK.
<v Gad Elmaleh>好的。

00:19:24.690 --> 00:19:28.500
<v Jerry Seinfeld>All right? And then I'm going to England, and I'm going to write some plays.
<v Jerry Seinfeld>好吗？然后我要去英国，我要写一些戏剧。

00:19:28.500 --> 00:19:30.290
<v Gad Elmaleh>OK.
<v Gad Elmaleh>好的。

00:19:30.290 --> 00:19:34.350
<v Jerry Seinfeld>Right? And then I'm going to go to America, and I'm going to do stand-up comedy.
<v Jerry Seinfeld>对吧？然后我要去美国，我要做单口喜剧。

00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:35.210
<v Gad Elmaleh>Yeah, so--
<v Gad Elmaleh>是的，所以——

00:19:35.210 --> 00:19:40.590
<v Jerry Seinfeld>Those people are used to the best.
<v Jerry Seinfeld>那些人习惯了最好的。

00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:41.060
<v Gad Elmaleh>OK.
<v Gad Elmaleh>好的。

00:19:41.060 --> 00:19:41.870
<v Jerry Seinfeld>You with me?
<v Jerry Seinfeld>你明白我的意思吗？

00:19:41.870 --> 00:19:42.810
<v Gad Elmaleh>I'm with you.
<v Gad Elmaleh>我明白了。

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:47.820
<v Jerry Seinfeld>So if it was me-- honestly, if it was me and I had what you had in France, I would just--
<v Jerry Seinfeld>所以如果是我的话——老实说，如果是我的话，我有你在法国拥有的那些东西，我只会——

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:49.140
<v Gad Elmaleh>You would stay.
<v Gad Elmaleh>你会留下来。

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:55.500
<v Jerry Seinfeld>I'm good. But I admire your ambition. I admire it.
<v Jerry Seinfeld>我很满足。但我钦佩你的雄心壮志。我钦佩它。

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:57.310
<v Gad Elmaleh>This is-- yeah. I like to hear that.
<v Gad Elmaleh>这很好——是的。我喜欢听这个。

00:19:57.310 --> 00:20:09.430
<v Jerry Seinfeld>I admire that. Yes. Yes. You have to be comfortable that it's going to hurt.
<v Jerry Seinfeld>我钦佩这一点。是的。是的。你必须习惯它会带来痛苦。

00:20:09.430 --> 00:20:42.530
<v Zack McDermott>This isn't the first unlikely quest Gad's embarked on. He's one of the people who brought stand-up comedy to France. Amazingly, it didn't exist there at all until about 15 years ago. Gad says they had no comedy clubs. Before then, he was a kind of entertainer that doesn't really exist here. He did these theatrical one-man shows, playing all sorts of characters, singing songs. But at some point, he became obsessed with the American style of comedy and started addressing his audience directly, telling them jokes just like we do here in the States-- in other words, stand-up.
<v Zack McDermott>这不是加德踏上的第一次不太可能的冒险。他是把单口喜剧带到法国的人之一。令人惊讶的是，直到大约15年前，那里根本不存在这种表演。加德说他们没有喜剧俱乐部。在那之前，他是那种我们这里并不真正存在的艺人。他做那种戏剧性的一人秀，扮演各种角色，唱着歌。但在某个时候，他开始沉迷于美式喜剧风格，并开始直接面对观众，像我们这里在美国那样给他们讲笑话——换句话说，单口喜剧。

00:20:42.530 --> 00:20:55.080
<v Gad Elmaleh>It was so new. People were like, oh, wow. What is this? He's talking to us. They were not used to it. No pigeon, no rope, no hats, no wigs.
<v Gad Elmaleh>太新了。人们就像，哦，哇。这是什么？他在跟我们说话。他们不习惯这样。没有鸽子，没有绳子，没有帽子，没有假发。

00:20:55.080 --> 00:20:59.850
<v Sean Cole>You're not being metaphorical. There were actual pigeons and ropes and wigs in your act before?
<v Sean Cole>你不是在用比喻吧。你在表演之前真的有鸽子、绳子和假发？

00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:00.180
<v Gad Elmaleh>No.
<v Gad Elmaleh>不。

00:21:00.180 --> 00:21:01.300
<v Sean Cole>You are being metaphorical?
<v Sean Cole>你是在用比喻？

00:21:01.300 --> 00:21:31.050
<v Gad Elmaleh>Yeah. It is just because this is how American comedians make fun of me sometimes. Like, oh, how is your show in France? Do you do mime and have like a pigeon and magic tricks? Because this is the stereotype. And if I want to talk about the stereotype we have on American comedians, I would say a guy standing up who doesn't move at all with a mic, a little depressed, and say jokes, jokes, jokes, jokes, jokes.
<v Gad Elmaleh>是的。这只是因为美国喜剧演员有时是这样取笑我的。比如，哦，你在法国的演出怎么样？你做哑剧，还有像鸽子和魔术吗？因为这是刻板印象。如果我想谈谈我们对美国喜剧演员的刻板印象，我会说是一个站在那里一动不动拿着麦克风的人，有点抑郁，然后讲笑话，笑话，笑话，笑话，笑话。

00:21:31.050 --> 00:21:58.430
<v Zack McDermott>But his French act is still very different from an American stand-up act. In his hour-plus long French specials, he plays piano and guitar and beats on his guitar like a bongo. And there are special guests. It's a boogaloo of a production-- everything but the pigeons. He tells a couple stories about his kid. He's got two-- one with Grace Kelly's granddaughter. He does a lot of observational humor. But he's 10 times more physical in his French act. He's practically performing the scene.
<v Zack McDermott>但他的法语表演与美国单口表演仍然有很大不同。在他一小时以上的法语专场中，他弹钢琴和吉他，还像敲邦戈鼓一样拍打吉他。还有特别嘉宾。这是一个热闹的制作——除了鸽子，什么都有。他讲了几个关于他孩子的段子。他有两个——一个是跟格蕾丝·凯利的孙女生的。他做很多观察式幽默。但他法语表演中的肢体动作要多出10倍。他几乎是在现场表演那个场景。

00:21:58.430 --> 00:22:03.890
<v Gad Elmaleh>[SPEAKING FRENCH]
<v Gad Elmaleh>[说法语]

00:22:03.890 --> 00:22:14.160
<v Zack McDermott>He's saying on an airplane, when they hand out little towelettes that smell good, you're like, yeah! I'm going to live the moment. Then he sits in a chair and acts out the part of an ecstatic passenger.
<v Zack McDermott>他说在飞机上，当他们分发闻起来不错的湿巾时，你就像，耶！我要活在当下。然后他坐在椅子上，表现出一个狂喜乘客的样子。

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:15.380
<v Gad Elmaleh>[EXCITED SNIFFING]
<v Gad Elmaleh>[兴奋的嗅探声]

00:22:15.380 --> 00:22:17.990
<v Gad Elmaleh>[MUTTERING]
<v Gad Elmaleh>[咕哝声]

00:22:17.990 --> 00:22:24.070
<v Gad Elmaleh>[SINGING IN FRENCH]
<v Gad Elmaleh>[用法语唱歌]

00:22:24.070 --> 00:22:28.640
<v Zack McDermott>So how do you translate that into American? Gad says you can't.
<v Zack McDermott>那么你如何把那个翻译成美式英语呢？加德说你不能。

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:38.760
<v Gad Elmaleh>I immediately, very quickly, realized that translating my French jokes into English was not the thing to do-- the good thing to do.
<v Gad Elmaleh>我很快，非常快地意识到，把我的法语笑话翻译成英语不是要做的事——不是正确要做的事。

00:22:38.760 --> 00:22:39.560
<v Sean Cole>Did you try that?
<v Sean Cole>你试过吗？

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:40.670
<v Gad Elmaleh>I tried.
<v Gad Elmaleh>我试过。

00:22:40.670 --> 00:22:54.030
<v Zack McDermott>And failed-- kind of spectacularly, in some cases. A lot of the stuff in his French act just doesn't travel well. Only a couple bits survived. He had to drop all the jokes about people freaking out when they meet him because he's so famous.
<v Zack McDermott>然后失败了——在某些情况下，相当壮观地失败了。他法语表演中的很多东西翻译过去效果不好。只有几个梗活了下来。他不得不放弃所有关于人们在见到他时因为他太出名而抓狂的笑话。

00:22:54.030 --> 00:23:15.960
<v Zack McDermott>Then there's the stuff he had to drop because of the obvious cultural barriers, or because of the vagaries of either language. For instance, he had this joke. He's mortified talking about it now, but it was about a common expression in French. French people, when someone breaks into their house, they say, je me sens violee. I feel like I've been violated. Except the word "violee" in French also means rape.
<v Zack McDermott>然后还有一些他因为明显的文化障碍，或者因为两种语言的变幻莫测而不得不放弃的东西。例如，他有一个笑话。他现在谈论它觉得很尴尬，但这是关于法语中一个常见的表达。法国人在有人闯入他们家时会说，“je me sens violee”。我感觉我被侵犯了。除了“violee”这个词在法语中也意味着强奸。

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:48.250
<v Gad Elmaleh>But in French, it's not as shocking as rape. So I didn't know that. So what I said-- I said, you know what guys? We have an expression. When people get burglarized, they say, I felt I was raped. [GASP] And it was like terrible, like ice, like silence. And I was like, oh my god. What did I say? I should not say that.
<v Gad Elmaleh>但在法语中，它没有强奸那么令人震惊。所以我不知道。所以我说的——我说，你知道吗伙计们？我们有一个表达。当人们被入室盗窃时，他们会说，我感觉我被强奸了。[吸气声] 然后就像可怕，就像冰，就像沉默。我当时想，天哪。我说了什么？我不应该说那个。

00:23:48.250 --> 00:24:05.230
<v Zack McDermott>Gad's next challenge, he had to recalibrate the pacing of his entire act. Turns out, comedy in France is like everything in France-- laid back, laissez-faire. They kind of ease into jokes the way they take a long time to eat dinner. He actually has a joke about that. Whereas in America--
<v Zack McDermott>加德的下一个挑战，他不得不重新校准他整场表演的节奏。事实证明，法国的喜剧就像法国的一切一样——轻松，自由放任。他们有点像吃晚餐那样慢慢地进入笑话。他实际上有一个关于这个的笑话。而美国——

00:24:05.230 --> 00:24:24.420
<v Gad Elmaleh>The structure of the phrases are shorter and sharper-- sharper? So what I've been doing every day is cutting the fat. I remember, when I first started to do the clubs, I was-- oh my god. I had long setups.
<v Gad Elmaleh>短语的结构更短更尖锐——尖锐？所以我每天都在做的是剔除脂肪。我记得，当我刚开始在俱乐部演出时，我——天哪。我有很长的铺垫。

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:47.900
<v Gad Elmaleh>OK. So I-- yeah. I was walking, and I said, I'm going to go to that store, so I went to that store. And the guy came up to me and said-- oh, my god. And I was with some friends-- like good friends-- and they said, Gad, stop that. Just go right away. This guy in a store told me this. I thought he was-- boom, boom, bam, bheem. Punchline. That's it. Stop with the whole pigeon thing.
<v Gad Elmaleh>好的。所以我——是的。我走着，我说，我要去那家店，所以我去了那家店。然后那家伙走过来对我说——天哪。我和一些朋友——像好朋友——在一起，他们说，加德，停下。直接进入主题。这家店里的家伙告诉了我这个。我以为他是——砰，砰，砰，砰，砰。包袱。就这样。别再提什么鸽子的事了。

00:24:47.900 --> 00:25:05.810
<v Zack McDermott>The hardest part of this whole endeavor is maybe what you'd guess-- the language. Gad's made a lot of progress since learning how to say Brian is in the kitchen as a kid. But after a few hours of speaking English, even just to us, Gad's exhausted. He says his brain turns to mush, and he just wants to speak French.
<v Zack McDermott>这整个努力中最困难的部分可能是你所猜测的那样——语言。自从小时候学会说“布莱恩在厨房里”以来，加德取得了很大进步。但在说了几个小时英语后，即使只是跟我们说话，加德也精疲力竭了。他说他的大脑变成了浆糊，他只想说法语。

00:25:05.810 --> 00:25:24.000
<v Zack McDermott>And a huge part of Gad's quest to become an American comedian is hard core language training. So much of comedy is about timing. If you're off by a beat or a syllable, you murder the joke. The joke is dead. So Gad's working with a language coach several times a week, dissecting his jokes, phrase by phrase, and syllable by syllable.
<v Zack McDermott>而加德成为美国喜剧演员的整个探索中，很大一部分是硬核语言训练。喜剧中很大一部分是关于时机的。如果你偏离了一个节拍或一个音节，你就杀死了这个笑话。这个笑话就死了。所以加德每周几次与语言教练一起工作，逐句逐句，逐音节逐音节地剖析他的笑话。

00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:26.900
<v Gad Elmaleh>Last night I did the American dream thing.
<v Gad Elmaleh>昨晚我做了美国梦的那段。

00:25:26.900 --> 00:25:27.460
<v Julia Lenardon>Yep.
<v Julia Lenardon>耶。

00:25:27.460 --> 00:25:31.190
<v Gad Elmaleh>And it was very hard. They didn't get it.
<v Gad Elmaleh>非常难。他们没听懂。

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:38.810
<v Zack McDermott>We sat in on Gad's session with his tutor, Julia Lenardon, in Gad's crash pad, i.e., a massive luxury apartment in Tribeca.
<v Zack McDermott>我们旁听了加德在加德临时住所——即翠贝卡区一套巨大的豪华公寓里——与他的导师朱莉娅·莱纳登的课程。

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:40.770
<v Julia Lenardon>Why? Because it's worked before. What happened?
<v Julia Lenardon>为什么？因为以前管用。发生了什么？

00:25:40.770 --> 00:25:54.160
<v Gad Elmaleh>Because it's the, I've wondered, and I was wondering, and I've always wondered. And my father used to take us to the ocean, or took us to the ocean. It's always-- I'm always confused.
<v Gad Elmaleh>因为是那个，“我一直在想”，和“我当时在想”，和“我一直都在想”。还有我父亲过去带我们去海边，或者是带我们去海边。总是——我总是很困惑。

00:25:54.160 --> 00:26:07.460
<v Zack McDermott>This joke is about how his father used to take him to the beach, point at the ocean, and tell Gad that, if you sail for days and nights, you will reach America. Gad wondered if any American father was standing on the shore pointing towards Morocco, saying the same thing.
<v Zack McDermott>这个笑话是关于他父亲过去常带他去海滩，指着大海，告诉加德，如果你航行几天几夜，你就会到达美国。加德想知道是否有美国父亲站在岸边指着摩洛哥，说着同样的话。

00:26:07.460 --> 00:26:10.360
<v Julia Lenardon>So he did it several times, or he did it once? That's what I need.
<v Julia Lenardon>所以他做了几次，还是他做了一次？这就是我需要的。

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:15.110
<v Gad Elmaleh>Every time we would go to the beach he would stand in front of the ocean and say this crazy thing.
<v Gad Elmaleh>每次我们去海滩，他都会站在大海前说这种疯狂的话。

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:16.990
<v Julia Lenardon>Then my father would take me.
<v Julia Lenardon>然后我父亲会带我。

00:26:16.990 --> 00:26:19.470
<v Gad Elmaleh>Yeah. My father would take me.
<v Gad Elmaleh>是的。我父亲会带我。

00:26:19.470 --> 00:26:20.140
<v Julia Lenardon>As opposed to--
<v Julia Lenardon>而不是——

00:26:20.140 --> 00:26:30.400
<v Gad Elmaleh>Maybe that's why, I don't know, they don't get it. I like it. It's not a crazy joke, but it's a poetic image. I always wondered if, on the other side of the ocean, there was an American father--
<v Gad Elmaleh>也许这就是为什么，我不知道，他们没听懂。我喜欢它。这不是一个疯狂的笑话，但它是一个诗意的形象。我一直想知道，在海洋的另一边，是否有一个美国父亲——

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:41.800
<v Julia Lenardon>Yeah. You see, that's the thing. An American father. That's the thing is you've got your keyword that you have to lift with pitch. And then you have to drop the next word because that's how we hear the contrast. The American father.
<v Julia Lenardon>是的。你看，这就是关键。一个美国父亲。关键是你有一个关键词，你必须用音高来强调它。然后你必须降低下一个词，因为那就是我们如何听到对比的方式。美国父亲。

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:44.090
<v Gad Elmaleh>An American father.
<v Gad Elmaleh>一个美国父亲。

00:26:44.090 --> 00:26:52.970
<v Gad Elmaleh>I'm not sure that on the other side of the ocean there was an American father [INAUDIBLE].
<v Gad Elmaleh>我不确定在海洋的另一边是否有一个美国父亲[听不清]。

00:26:52.970 --> 00:26:56.910
<v Gad Elmaleh>[LAUGHTER]
<v Gad Elmaleh>[笑声]

00:26:56.910 --> 00:27:24.080
<v Zack McDermott>For a while, Gad had this other joke that just wasn't working till Julia told Gad to say "vay-KAY-shun" instead of "VAY-kay-shun." If that doesn't work, she said, you can fire me. It worked. When you know how hard it is for him, watching him do an hour set in English looks like an Olympic feat. But of course, that's not how these things are scored.
<v Zack McDermott>有一段时间，加德有另一个笑话一直没效果，直到朱莉娅告诉加德说“vay-KAY-shun”而不是“VAY-kay-shun”。如果那不管用，她说，你可以开除我。管用了。当你了解对他来说有多难时，看他用英语做一小时的演出就像是一项奥林匹克成就。但当然，评分标准不是这样的。

00:27:24.080 --> 00:27:54.100
<v Zack McDermott>Gad's about a year into his new American life. And to be clear, because of his fame elsewhere and his connections, Gad's had about every conceivable unfair advantage a, quote, "fledgling comedian in America" could ask for. That show you heard at Joe's Pub was actually opening night of a six-month residency, three shows a week. No rookie comedian in New York gets that. They knew he could pack the room with French ex-pats. Gad's been on The Daily Show , The Nightly Show , Late Night with Seth Meyers . He did a set on Conan .
<v Zack McDermott>加德在他的美国新生活中大约过了一年。需要明确的是，因为他在其他地方的知名度和他人脉，加德拥有了几乎所有你所说的“美国新晋喜剧演员”所能要求的不公平优势。你在乔酒吧听到的那场演出实际上是六个月驻场表演的开幕之夜，每周三场演出。纽约没有新秀喜剧演员能得到那个。他们知道他能用法国外籍人士填满房间。加德上过《每日秀》、《夜间秀》、大卫·莱特曼的《深夜秀》。他在《柯南秀》上做了一段表演。

00:27:54.100 --> 00:28:15.720
<v Zack McDermott>And he's getting better. His set got tighter during his residency at Joe's Pub. And he might still have the occasional language misstep, but it's happening later in the show. His English is improving. And he just knows his way around the act now. When it works, Gad says, it means more to him than performing for his French fans who've seen his DVDs and know every joke and laugh at everything.
<v Zack McDermott>而且他越来越好了。他在乔酒吧驻场期间，他的段子变得更紧凑了。他可能偶尔还会有语言失误，但那是发生在演出后期。他的英语在进步。而且他现在知道怎么处理这场表演了。加德说，当它奏效时，这对他来说比为那些看过他DVD、知道每一个笑话并对一切都笑的法国粉丝表演更有意义。

00:28:15.720 --> 00:28:58.740
<v Gad Elmaleh>When American audience laugh at my joke, I mean, I feel I'm just, oh, I'm funny. I'm a funny man. I'm not that guy. And even in my everyday life, if I go to a store and I make someone laugh, like at the grocery store, I'm so happy because I'm like, oh, she doesn't know that I'm famous in France, and she just laughed so hard at my joke. I made a joke about, I don't know, cheese or whatever, and I asked her a question and I made this little face, and she was laughing-- really laughing-- and I'm like, oh, I'm a funny man. I'm a funny man.
<v Gad Elmaleh>当美国观众对我的笑话发笑时，我的意思是，我感觉我只是，哦，我很幽默。我是个幽默的人。我不是那个人。即使在我的日常生活中，如果我去商店，我让某人笑，比如在杂货店，我太开心了，因为我心想，哦，她不知道我在法国很有名，她只是被我的笑话逗笑了。我讲了一个关于，我不知道，奶酪还是什么的笑话，我问了她一个问题，我做了这个小表情，她就在笑——真正地在笑——我心想，哦，我真是个幽默的人。我真是个幽默的人。

00:28:58.740 --> 00:29:15.580
<v Gad Elmaleh>I love to be anonymous. I love-- I love it. And I hope it's not going to stay like this forever. [LAUGHS]
<v Gad Elmaleh>我喜欢默默无闻。我喜欢——我喜欢它。我希望它不会永远保持这样。[笑声]

00:29:15.580 --> 00:29:49.390
<v Zack McDermott>But does he really have a shot at becoming a name here in the US? To answer that, I turned to people who'd know-- some American comedy headliners. I talked to Colin Quinn, Ali Wong, Ron Funches, and Jeff Garlin. All of them watched the same video of a 15-minute set Gad did at the Comedy Cellar back in June. And they all agreed he's a pro. He could totally have a career here. But can he be great? They all said he's not there yet, and they were unanimous on what he needed to do next. There is one specific hole in his act all four pointed to. Here's Colin Quinn.
<v Zack McDermott>但他真的有希望在美国成为大人物吗？为了回答这个问题，我转向了那些会知道的人——一些美国喜剧头牌。我跟科林·奎因、黄艾丽、罗恩·芬奇斯和杰夫·加林聊过。他们都看了加德六月份在喜剧地窖做的15分钟表演的视频。他们都同意他是个专业人士。他完全可以在这里有事业。但他能成为伟大的人吗？他们都说他还没到那一步，而且他们一致认为他接下来需要做什么。他们四个人都指出了他表演中有一个具体的漏洞。这是科林·奎因。

00:29:49.390 --> 00:30:03.880
<v Colin Quinn>Talking about his perceptions of America. All that stuff is great. Love it, love it, love it-- for 20 minutes. Then I want to hear about his life, and I want to hear about France or Morocco. He's had this fascinating life. Let's [BLEEP] hear about it.
<v Colin Quinn>谈论他对美国的看法。所有这些东西都很棒。喜欢它，喜欢它，喜欢它——持续20分钟。然后我想听听他的生活，我想听听法国或摩洛哥的故事。他有过这种迷人的生活。让我们他妈的听听它。

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:22.980
<v Colin Quinn>The whole point of going to see a comedian is you want to see something that other comedians don't do. What about your country? I'm interested. It's all relatable. It's emotions, you know what I mean? My favorite expression-- now I get to use my favorite expression, which I'm always looking for an excuse. In the specific is the universal.
<v Colin Quinn>去看喜剧演员的全部意义在于你想看到其他喜剧演员不做的事情。你的国家呢？我很感兴趣。这都是可关联的。都是情感，你明白我的意思吗？我最喜欢的表达——现在我有机会使用我最喜欢的表达，我一直在找借口。在具体中就是普遍。

00:30:22.980 --> 00:30:27.130
<v Sean Cole>What would he have to do to go from this level that he's at right now to being great?
<v Sean Cole>他需要做什么才能从现在的水平达到伟大？

00:30:27.130 --> 00:30:29.710
<v Colin Quinn>He'd have to listen to what I just said.
<v Colin Quinn>他得听听我刚才说的。

00:30:29.710 --> 00:30:36.700
<v Zack McDermott>Being personal-- that's where American comedy's at right now. Ali Wong said something similar after she watched the video.
<v Zack McDermott>变得个人化——这就是美国喜剧现在的方向。黄艾丽在看了视频后也说了类似的话。

00:30:36.700 --> 00:30:44.040
<v Ali Wong>It was so interesting because you can tell that he's a really seasoned performer, and he's very comfortable on stage.
<v Ali Wong>这太有趣了，因为你可以看出他是一个非常老练的表演者，而且他在舞台上非常自在。

00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:50.110
<v Zack McDermott>But she said that, like a lot of rookie comedians in New York, the topics he gravitates toward are a little too generic.
<v Zack McDermott>但她说，像纽约的许多新晋喜剧演员一样，他倾向的主题有点太普通了。

00:30:50.110 --> 00:31:15.250
<v Ali Wong>You know, how hard it is to get an apartment, why women always want to commit, Indian cab drivers talk a lot on their phone. For me, when you're a headliner, those are people who kind of give a lot of their essence on stage. Like, I would love to know why the Jerry Seinfeld of France would want to do stand-up in America all of a sudden?
<v Ali Wong>你知道，租公寓有多难，为什么女人总是想承诺，印度出租车司机一直在电话里聊天。对我来说，当你是一名头牌时，那些人在舞台上给予了很多他们的本质。比如，我很想知道为什么法国的杰瑞·宋飞会突然想在美国做单口喜剧？

00:31:15.250 --> 00:31:23.310
<v Zack McDermott>Ron Funches agreed with Ali that the most obvious thing for Gad to talk about is one of the topics he thought he had to avoid on stage-- his fame.
<v Zack McDermott>罗恩·芬奇斯和艾丽一样同意，关于加德最明显的话题是他认为自己必须在舞台上避免的话题之一——他的名气。

00:31:23.310 --> 00:31:45.230
<v Ron Funches>This-- what's going on now, his fear of being like, oh, I'm a big deal in France, and now I'm in America, and I'm not that big of a deal. Tell me what that's about. Tell me how that feels. That's probably terrifying and also very brave of you to step out of your comfort zone. Tell me about that. Tell me what you think is funny about that. That would be very interesting to me.
<v Ron Funches>这——现在发生的事，他害怕被认为，哦，我在法国是个大人物，现在我在美国，我不是什么大人物。告诉我那是关于什么的。告诉我那是什么感觉。那可能很可怕，但也非常勇敢，因为你走出了舒适区。告诉我那些。告诉我你觉得其中哪些地方好笑。那对我来说会非常有趣。

00:31:45.230 --> 00:31:46.980
<v Gad Elmaleh>I think it's a good point.
<v Gad Elmaleh>我觉得这是个好观点。

00:31:46.980 --> 00:31:56.210
<v Zack McDermott>Again, here's Gad. We played him all the feedback, and he was a good sport about it, and he thought it was good advice. He really liked Ron's idea for how to talk about his fame.
<v Zack McDermott>再说一次，加德。我们把所有的反馈都放给他听，他对此很有体育精神，而且他认为这是很好的建议。他真的很喜欢罗恩关于如何谈论他名气的点子。

00:31:56.210 --> 00:32:18.620
<v Gad Elmaleh>His point of view is interesting. So maybe I should explain-- just tell them, guys, it's a new life, being famous somewhere and maybe sometimes I'm walking down the street in New York City and I don't get bothered. But it's also bizarre, you know? I want to go to some people and say, do you want a photo or something?
<v Gad Elmaleh>他的观点很有趣。所以也许我应该解释——只是告诉他们，伙计们，这是新生活，在某处出名，也许有时我在纽约街头走，没人烦我。但这也是怪诞的，你知道吗？我想去找些人说，你们想要合影吗？

00:32:18.620 --> 00:32:21.630
<v Sean Cole>[LAUGHS] Yeah. Bother me.
<v Sean Cole>[笑声] 是的。烦我吧。

00:32:21.630 --> 00:32:26.160
<v Gad Elmaleh>Do you want a photo of us? No. That would be good, no?
<v Gad Elmaleh>你想要跟我合影吗？不。因为我在法国很有名。是的，但那是在法国。那很有趣。我应该那样做。

00:32:26.160 --> 00:32:27.510
<v Zack McDermott>That's good.
<v Zack McDermott>那很好。

00:32:27.510 --> 00:32:41.240
<v Gad Elmaleh>Do you want a picture with me? No. Because I'm famous in France. Yeah, but that's in France. That's funny. I should do that.
<v Gad Elmaleh>你想要跟我拍张照片吗？不。因为我在法国很有名。是的，但那是在法国。那很有趣。我应该那样做。

00:32:41.240 --> 00:32:58.470
<v Zack McDermott>But the most pointed criticism was from Jeff Garlin. You might recognize Jeff as Larry David's sidekick/manager on Curb Your Enthusiasm , but he's got 30 years in stand-up. He worked with Jon Stewart and Denis Leary on their stand-up specials, and he's a comic's comic. We weren't talking for very long before he just cut to the chase.
<v Zack McDermott>但最尖锐的批评来自杰夫·加林。你可能认出杰夫是《抑制热情》里拉里·戴维的搭档/经理，但他有30年的单口喜剧经验。他在乔恩·斯图尔特和丹尼斯·利里的单口专场里跟他们合作过，他是喜剧演员中的喜剧演员。我们没聊多久，他就直接切入重点。

00:32:58.470 --> 00:32:59.970
<v Jeff Garlin>He does not make me laugh.
<v Jeff Garlin>他没让我笑。

00:32:59.970 --> 00:33:01.360
<v Sean Cole>Huh.
<v Sean Cole>呵。

00:33:01.360 --> 00:33:24.900
<v Jeff Garlin>Yes. But I find him unbelievably charming. I think he's charismatic. He's charming. I could see him becoming very successful here. The audience was under his spell. He has a spell. There's a craft to stand-up comedy as well as an art, and he is a master at the craft. I don't look at him as much of an artist.
<v Jeff Garlin>是的。但我发现他难以置信地迷人。我觉得他很有魅力。他很迷人。我能看到他在这里变得非常成功。观众都在他的魔咒之下。他有一种魔力。单口喜剧既有艺术也有工艺，而他是工艺大师。我对他作为艺术家的看法没那么多。

00:33:24.900 --> 00:33:30.620
<v Zack McDermott>Jeff said to be an artist, he'd have to be more passionate about the stuff he's talking about. It doesn't matter what it is.
<v Zack McDermott>杰夫说要成为艺术家，他必须对他谈论的东西更充满激情。那是什么东西并不重要。

00:33:30.620 --> 00:33:50.530
<v Jeff Garlin>Just tell me what you care about. He doesn't care about Starbucks on every corner. That's a bunch of crap. And let me tell you, if he's not bored doing that every night, there's something wrong with him because I'd want to kill myself. What do you care about, Gad? That's what I'm saying. What do you care about?
<v Jeff Garlin>只要告诉我你在乎什么。他根本不在乎每个角落的星巴克。那是一堆废话。让我告诉你，如果他每晚做那个不觉得无聊，那他肯定有什么毛病，因为我要是那样我会想自杀。你在乎什么，加德？这就是我在说的。你在乎什么？

00:33:50.530 --> 00:34:18.270
<v Gad Elmaleh>Interesting. Wow. Yeah, yeah. Wow. Now that I-- the English is OK, I should maybe be more fragile, even though it's in English, and maybe trust what I think, trust what I feel. Wow. Now it's getting really interesting.
<v Gad Elmaleh>有趣。哇。是的，是的。哇。现在——英语还可以，我也许应该更脆弱一点，即使是在英语中，也许相信我的想法，相信我的感觉。哇。现在变得真有趣。

00:34:18.270 --> 00:34:24.360
<v Zack McDermott>Gad couldn't stop thinking about this. 20 minutes later, he just interrupted Sean mid-sentence to come back to it.
<v Zack McDermott>加德无法停止思考这一点。20分钟后，他只是打断了肖恩的话，回到这个话题。

00:34:24.360 --> 00:34:27.510
<v Sean Cole>So I'm just wondering how you see yourself having--
<v Sean Cole>所以我只是在想你是怎么看待你自己拥有——

00:34:27.510 --> 00:34:28.930
<v Gad Elmaleh>Now let me tell you something.
<v Gad Elmaleh>现在让我告诉你一件事。

00:34:28.930 --> 00:34:29.380
<v Sean Cole>Yes.
<v Sean Cole>是的。

00:34:29.380 --> 00:34:39.010
<v Gad Elmaleh>We had those five minutes of their critiques and the thinking, and now my brain is already working on the new material.
<v Gad Elmaleh>我们有了那五分钟的批评和思考，现在我的大脑已经在研究新素材了。

00:34:39.010 --> 00:34:39.720
<v Sean Cole>Wow.
<v Sean Cole>哇。

00:34:39.720 --> 00:34:48.360
<v Gad Elmaleh>It's a little revolution today for me. So it's really interesting. It's a shock, but it's in a good way.
<v Gad Elmaleh>对我来说今天是一场小革命。所以这真的很有趣。这是一个冲击，但是以好的方式。

00:34:48.360 --> 00:34:49.450
<v Sean Cole>What are you thinking about?
<v Sean Cole>你在想什么？

00:34:49.450 --> 00:35:15.660
<v Gad Elmaleh>I'm thinking that, when I will go to clubs, I really want to go there. I really want to try to talk more about my roots, Morocco, friends, and not only comparing-- not only saying, oh, you guys do this? We do that. You guys do this? We do that. Man, that's really, really a good shock that you gave me today.
<v Gad Elmaleh>我在想，当我以后去俱乐部时，我真的想去那里。我真的想尝试多谈谈我的根源，摩洛哥，朋友，而不只是比较——不只是说，哦，你们这样做？我们做那样。你们这样做？我们做那样。伙计，那真是，真是你今天给我的一个好的冲击。

00:35:15.660 --> 00:35:38.130
<v Gad Elmaleh>There will be a before and after today. Really. What do you care about? I remember, what do you care about? What does he care about? I care about people who say what does he care about. Merci.
<v Gad Elmaleh>今天之后会有之前和之后。真的。你在乎什么？我记得，你在乎什么？他在乎什么？我在乎那些问他“他在乎什么”的人。谢谢。

00:35:38.130 --> 00:35:52.100
<v Zack McDermott>I saw Gad at the Comedy Cellar one more time after that interview, two weeks ago. He hadn't changed up his set much. He wasn't getting into the personal stuff yet. I asked him why, and he said, revolutions don't happen overnight.
<v Zack McDermott>那次采访后两周，我又在喜剧地窖见过加德一次。他并没有做太多改动。他还没开始接触个人化素材。我问他为什么，他说，革命不是一夜之间发生的。

00:35:52.100 --> 00:36:31.920
<v Zack McDermott>The comedians I talked to were adamant. For Gad to come up with the kind of material he's going to need to be great in America-- the personal stuff, the stuff he really cares about-- the only way to develop that is to do painful sets on stage where he tries out all kinds of stuff and lets himself bomb. In France, he doesn't do that. And Gad told me it goes against all his instincts-- against 22 years of training-- but he's going to have to override that instinct. He's going to have to embrace bombing, learn to fail at comedy at a whole new level, if he's going to succeed here. It's a concept that's totally foreign to him.
<v Zack McDermott>我聊过的那些喜剧演员很坚定。为了让加德想出他要在美国变得伟大所需要的素材——个人化的东西，他真正关心的东西——发展的唯一途径是在舞台上进行痛苦的演出，尝试各种东西并让自己搞砸。在法国，他不那么做。加德告诉我，这违反了他所有的直觉——违反了22年的训练——但他必须覆盖那种直觉。他必须拥抱搞砸，学习在单口喜剧上以全新的水平失败，如果他想在这里成功的话。这对他来说是个完全陌生的概念。

00:36:31.920 --> 00:36:34.740
<v Ira Glass>Zack McDermott in New York.
<v Ira Glass>扎克·麦克德莫特在纽约。

00:36:34.740 --> 00:36:49.070
<v Ira Glass>Gad Elmaleh is about to embark on a European tour, performing in French-- you know, for a change. American shows resume in the new year. We have some clips of him performing in English on our website, ThisAmericanLife.org. In February, he'll be in Carnegie Hall.
<v Ira Glass>加德·艾尔马莱即将开始欧洲巡演，表演法语——你知道，换换口味。美国演出将在新的一年恢复。我们的网站ThisAmericanLife.org上有他用英语表演的片段。二月份，他将在卡内基音乐厅演出。

00:36:49.070 --> 00:37:31.380
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "FAMOUS IN FRANCE" BY JOHN MCDERMOTT AND MICHAEL P. SMITH]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 约翰·麦克德莫特和迈克尔·P·史密斯的《FAMOUS IN FRANCE》]

00:37:31.380 --> 00:37:47.560
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, a little dog who lives in an apartment and whose favorite toy is called a Giggle Ball goes tete-a-tete with the wily creatures that brought us the bubonic plague. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>即将到来，一只住在公寓里、最喜欢的玩具叫“咯咯球”的小狗，与给我们带来黑死病的狡猾生物进行一对一的较量。那是在一分钟后，来自芝加哥公共广播电台，我们的节目继续。

00:37:47.560 --> 00:37:59.340
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, Becoming a Badger, stories of people trying to become something totally foreign, trying to see the world through new eyes.
<v Ira Glass>这是《This American Life》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们选择一个主题，带给你关于这个主题的不同种类的故事。今天的节目，“成为一只獾”，关于人们试图成为完全陌生事物的故事，试图通过新的眼睛看世界。

00:37:59.340 --> 00:38:16.460
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "THE 5TH QUARTER" BY THE MADHATTERS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 疯帽子乐队的《THE 5TH QUARTER》]

00:38:16.460 --> 00:38:29.560
<v Ira Glass>Those are the MadHatters from Madison, Wisconsin, singing a fight song from the University of Wisconsin, whose football team, of course, is the Badgers. And with that, we have arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, The Curious Incident of the Dog in the Nighttime.
<v Ira Glass>那是来自威斯康星州麦迪逊的疯帽子乐队，在唱威斯康星大学的战斗歌，威斯康星大学的足球队，当然就是獾队。随着那个，我们已经到达了节目的第二幕。第二幕，“狗在夜间的离奇事件”。

00:38:29.560 --> 00:38:39.900
<v Ira Glass>So we heard at the beginning of our show about an Oxford scholar who was trying to understand what it's like to be an animal. Now, in this act, we hear about an animal trying to understand what it's like to be an animal.
<v Ira Glass>所以我们在节目开头听到了一个关于牛津学者试图理解成为动物是什么感觉。现在，在这一幕中，我们听到关于一个动物试图理解成为动物是什么感觉。

00:38:39.900 --> 00:39:03.740
<v Ira Glass>I sometimes have this thought about my own dog, which is his feet never touch the earth. Like, they never touch grass and dirt. He lives in a city. He lives on sidewalk and in a tiny apartment 40 feet above the ground. Our producers Zoe Chace and Emmanuel Dzotsi have this story of a dog-- an urban dog-- getting more in touch with the doggy parts of himself.
<v Ira Glass>我有时对自己狗有这样一个想法，就是他的脚从不触及大地。就像，它们从不碰草和泥土。他住在城市里。他住在人行道上，住在离地面40英尺高的一间微小的公寓里。我们的制作人佐伊·蔡斯和伊曼纽尔·佐齐有这样一个关于一只狗的故事——一只城市狗——更多地接触他自己身上狗的部分。

00:39:03.740 --> 00:39:10.560
<v Zoe Chace>When we set out to do this story, Ira was like, you've got to put a mic on our main character. His name is Ray Ray.
<v Zoe Chace>当我们着手做这个故事时，艾拉说，你得给我们的主角戴上麦克风。他的名字是雷雷。

00:39:10.560 --> 00:39:15.360
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Ray Ray is a dog-- a terrier. We attached a tiny microphone to his collar.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>雷雷是一只狗——梗犬。我们在他的项圈上装了一个微小的麦克风。

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:24.340
<v Zoe Chace>Ray Ray, you're going to be my first radio dog. Ray, say something.
<v Zoe Chace>雷雷，你要成为我的第一只无线电狗。雷，说点什么。

00:39:24.340 --> 00:39:28.190
<v Ira Glass>A ball is thrown down the hallway. This is what it's like to be Ray Ray.
<v Ira Glass>一个球被扔进走廊。这就是成为雷雷的感觉。

00:39:28.190 --> 00:39:31.890
<v Ira Glass>[SCAMPERING]
<v Ira Glass>[小跑声]

00:39:31.890 --> 00:39:37.980
<v Ira Glass>Some mix of eternal optimism and desperation to catch the thing that is getting away from you.
<v Ira Glass>某种永恒的乐观和捕获正在逃离你的东西的绝望的混合体。

00:39:37.980 --> 00:39:43.910
<v Ira Glass>[SCAMPERING]
<v Ira Glass>[小跑声]

00:39:43.910 --> 00:39:46.590
<v Ira Glass>The owner of the dog in question is Judy.
<v Ira Glass>这只狗的主人是朱迪。

00:39:46.590 --> 00:39:51.630
<v Judy>Sit. No more. We have to save it for later.
<v Judy>坐。别动。我们得留着以后用。

00:39:51.630 --> 00:40:12.890
<v Zoe Chace>She's the kind of old-school New Yorker that you meet less and less. She was born on the Lower East Side, grew up in Queens, doesn't have a car, doesn't really leave the city. And she's lived in the same tiny, two-room apartment for 30 years. She's a dog person. She lives with this big, quiet greyhound and Ray Ray, the hyper little terrier. He's a rescue. Before that--
<v Zoe Chace>她是那种你越来越少见的旧派纽约人。她出生在下东区，在皇后区长大，没有车，也不怎么离开城市。她在那间微小的两居室公寓里住了30年。她是个爱狗的人。她和这只大而安静的灰狗以及雷雷，那只超级活跃的小梗犬住在一起。他是一只救援犬。在那之前——

00:40:12.890 --> 00:40:26.860
<v Judy>I think he was in a home in New Jersey because he had no street skills at all. And his pads-- like he had never walked on concrete. His pads were like pink baby pads.
<v Judy>我想他是在新泽西的一个收容所里，因为他根本没有任何街道技能。他的脚垫——就像他从没在混凝土上走过。他的脚垫就像粉红色的婴儿脚垫。

00:40:26.860 --> 00:40:44.550
